DOCS["halftone-newsprint-geometry"].update({
    "description": "Build a multicolor or Black-only halftone from one shared matrix of variable-size circles or squares.",
    "intro": "Choose Halftone Newsprint under Geometry Style after selecting a compatible Image Style. It samples the prepared artwork on a regular physical grid and emits circles or squares. By default, each mark remains on the Rasterizer swatch layer owning that location. Black Only instead sends every mark to the official Black layer for a conventional monochrome halftone.",
    "sections": [
        ("Multicolor and Black-only output", ["Leave Black Only disabled to retain the existing color-owned matrix, where each position uses its matching Rasterizer swatch. Enable Black Only to keep the same dot centers and luminance-driven sizes while assigning every mark to the official Black layer.", "For a LightBurn project, the selected Material Library or saved palette must assign the official Black swatch to a Cut Setting Entry. SVG-only jobs can use the built-in Black layer without laser settings."]),
        ("Dot Size Source", ["Source Brightness sizes each mark from the original artwork's grayscale value at that cell, preserving tone even when neighboring cells share the same output swatch.", "Seeded Variation assigns repeatable pseudo-random sizes between Minimum and Maximum Dot Ratio. The same artwork and Seed reproduce the same distribution; changing Seed creates another arrangement. Use it when swatch color should carry the image while dot sizes provide texture rather than tone."]),
        ("Dot size and spacing", ["Cell Size MM controls the fixed center-to-center spacing of the matrix. Minimum and Maximum Dot Ratio control the smallest and largest mark sizes inside those cells. Tone Curve and Contrast reshape how source brightness maps between those limits.", "Non-Black Dot Density multiplies the engraved area of color marks after tone calculation. Its default of 2.30 increases non-Black coverage while leaving Black unchanged, and every mark remains confined to its matrix cell. Black Only changes layer ownership and uses the original tone-calculated sizes.", "Closely spaced large dots produce denser dark regions, while smaller marks expose more unengraved material in lighter regions."]),
        ("Physical testing", ["A dot that is valid geometry may still be too small for a particular laser setting, spot size, focus, material, or oxide-color process. Begin with a small test and increase Cell Size MM or Minimum Dot Ratio when the smallest marks do not reproduce consistently."]),
    ],
    "related": ["geometry-styles", "color-layers", "reduce-lightburn-object-count"],
})

DOCS["optical-color-mix-filter"].update({
    "description": "Keep ordinary vector regions for colors represented by available Rasterizer swatches and use deterministic two-swatch dot mixtures only where they improve the source-color match.",
    "intro": "Optical Color Mix samples the original source image before ordinary one-swatch color quantization can discard intermediate colors. For each mixing cell, it compares the target source color with every available direct swatch and with the dot ratios representable by every pair of available swatches. When a direct palette match is adequate, all ordinary processed swatch geometry in that cell remains continuous vector geometry. Only a cell better represented by a two-swatch calculation becomes non-overlapping marks on the component swatches' LightBurn layers.",
    "sections": [
        ("What the filter is doing", ["This is a hybrid of ordinary Rasterizer vectors and spatial color mixing. Areas already represented adequately by available palette swatches remain continuous filled vector regions. Viewed closely, only the unavailable-color areas reveal individual component-color marks; viewed from farther away, the eye may average those marks into another perceived color.", "Direct Color Preference controls how much improvement a calculated mixture must provide before the filter replaces a cell's normal vectors with the extra dot-layer complexity."]),
        ("Matching models", ["Perceptual Lab is the recommended default. It mixes the declared swatch colors in linear-light RGB, converts the predicted mixture to Lab, and selects the candidate with the smallest normalized perceptual difference from the source target. Linear RGB compares optical intensities directly. Creative HSV compares hue, saturation, and value and may produce more stylized choices.", "The Mixing Model selector is categorical rather than numeric and is therefore described here instead of in the numeric control table below."]),
        ("Mixing cells and dots", ["Dot Pitch sets the physical center spacing. Mixing Cell Dots sets how many dot rows and columns form one ratio cell: a 4-by-4 cell provides 16 positions and therefore represents pair ratios in sixteenth-sized steps. Dot Size Ratio changes mark coverage without allowing neighboring marks to overlap.", "Smaller pitch can improve blending but creates more vector objects and may demand marks near or below the effective laser spot size. Larger mixing cells provide more precise ratios but make color transitions coarser. Begin with a small test at the intended viewing distance."]),
        ("Uncalibrated correction controls", ["Hue Shift, Saturation Gain, and Brightness Gamma alter the predicted appearance of every available swatch before mixture selection. Dot Influence changes how strongly the minority swatch is predicted to affect a mixture compared with its geometric coverage. Neutral Bias discourages or favors Black and gray participation. These are global approximations, not substitutes for measured per-swatch calibration.", "Pattern Seed changes the repeatable arrangement of the same component ratios. Square Dots replaces circular marks with squares while preserving exclusive layer ownership."]),
        ("Physical limitations", ["Declared RGB values do not fully predict oxide color, diffraction color, spectral reflectance, viewing-angle changes, illumination, focus, heat accumulation, scan-length behavior, or how a setting developed for a large fill behaves on a small mark. Optical Color Mix can expand useful perceived transitions between available swatches, but it cannot guarantee a requested color or synthesize colors outside the practical gamut of the engraved component marks.", "Open the generated project in LightBurn, verify that every component layer has the intended tested setting, engrave a small sample, and adjust the correction controls from the physical result before producing large artwork."]),
    ],
    "related": ["abstract-filters", "abstract-filter-reference", "color-layers", "reduce-lightburn-object-count"],
})

DOCS["krasnow-grating-geometry"] = _page(
    "Krasnow Grating Geometry Style",
    "Create open-path diffraction-grating geometry after compatible image processing.",
    "Krasnow Grating is a Geometry Style. It follows the selected compatible Image Style and can also be assigned only to selected swatches through Choose by swatch.",
    [],
    ["geometry-styles", "color-layers", "diffraction-gratings", "holographic-calibration"],
)
DOCS["krasnow-grating-geometry"].update({
    "description": "Create open-path diffraction-grating artwork based on Ben Krasnow's method by using the cut settings stored in the selected material's Fauxlographic entry as a machine-specific 1.00 µm calibration anchor and scaling the output-layer speeds to produce a range of pulse pitches.",
    "intro": "Krasnow Grating Geometry Style follows Rasterizer's image resizing, palette quantization, and color-region cleanup stages, then rebuilds selected geometry as patches of parallel open lines. Source-image luminance controls each patch's line angle, while source-image hue and the selected fauxlogram gradient determine which diffraction carrier receives that patch.",
    "sections": [
        ("What remains unchanged", ["The source image is resized to the selected processing dimensions, quantized against the available Rasterizer swatches backed by matching cut settings in the selected material, and cleaned into closed color regions by the selected compatible Image Style before Krasnow geometry is built."]),
        ("Black geometry", ["Rasterizer separately reduces the resized source image to an adaptive two-color mask, converts the darker portion into merged closed geometry, and places that geometry on the Black layer. This Black geometry remains closed and is not replaced with diffraction-grating lines. The preset works best when Black appears sparingly in the source image, such as outlines or small dark details, rather than large filled regions. The preset does not generate additional Black background geometry or duplicate the non-Black grating paths onto the Black layer; the Black layer contains only the closed geometry produced by the adaptive source-image mask."]),
        ("Cell shape", ["Cell Shape selects the gap-free grid that clips each local grating cell. Square is the default and preserves the established Krasnow output. Hexagon creates a regular honeycomb, Triangle uses alternating equilateral cells, and Diamond / Rhombus uses staggered rotated squares. Cells share boundaries without intentional gaps or overlaps.", "Patch Size is the square width and height, the hexagon flat-to-flat height, the triangle side length, or both point-to-point diagonals of the Diamond / Rhombus. Triangle therefore creates more cells and can produce more geometry than the other shapes at the same Patch Size. Cell Shape does not change hue mapping, grating angle, line spacing, or the calibrated microscopic diffraction pitch."]),
        ("Open-path geometry", ["Each non-Black color region is divided into local patches, and every patch is rebuilt as parallel open lines clipped to that region's prepared boundary. The defaults match Ben's 0.4 mm patch size and 0.06 mm line spacing. By default, source-image luminance maps from -90 degrees for the darkest values to +90 degrees for the lightest values, matching Ben's grayscale-to-angle convention.", "Every available non-Black grating layer is exported in LightBurn Line mode because the hatch-like pattern has already been constructed as vector paths. The non-Black grating layers therefore contain open paths, while source-derived Black remains closed geometry on the Black layer."]),
        ("Fauxlographic calibration anchor", ["The selected material must contain a Material Library entry whose Description, or whose stored cut setting's internal name, contains \"Fauxlographic.\" Rasterizer treats the speed and frequency stored in the matched Fauxlographic entry as a machine-specific combination that produces a calibrated 1.00 µm pulse pitch (usually an iridescent color setting whose apparent color changes with the lighting or viewing angle, like an oil slick or soap bubble). Rasterizer loads the matched Fauxlographic entry once as a template, then independently copies it onto every available non-Black output layer that remains after normal Rasterizer swatch and Material Library matching.", "Each clone retains its output layer's original LightBurn ID, color, and name. Power, frequency, pulse width, passes, and the other copied laser settings remain equal to the Fauxlographic anchor; only each output layer's speed is adjusted to produce its target pulse pitch."]),
        ("How correction is applied", ["For source colors above the Saturation Cutoff, Rasterizer applies the selected Hue Rotation and converts the rotated hue through Ben Krasnow's inverse 15-to-240 control mapping to produce a grating-layer selection offset. Rasterizer adds the top-to-bottom correction ramp defined by Gradient Top, Gradient Bottom, and Gradient Curve to the hue-derived offset, then limits the combined value to the 0-to-255 range. Rasterizer divides the combined value across however many non-Black grating layers are available and assigns those levels in the layers' native LightBurn order. Source colors at or below the Saturation Cutoff are treated as neutral and receive only the vertical correction ramp, without a hue-derived offset."]),
        ("Pitch extrapolation", ["Pulse pitch is the distance between successive laser pulses along the scan direction and is determined by speed divided by frequency; increasing speed or decreasing frequency increases that distance. With frequency fixed and Speed Spread at its default of 1, each target layer uses: layer speed = Fauxlographic anchor speed × target pitch / 1.00 µm. When multiple non-Black grating layers are available, their target pitches are distributed evenly in LightBurn order from approximately 0.55 to 1.55 µm; if only one is available, it remains at the 1.00 µm anchor.", "Speed Spread controls how far layer speeds diverge from the anchor: speed = anchor speed × [1 + Speed Spread × (target pitch / 1.00 µm − 1)]. A value of 0 gives every layer the anchor speed, 1 preserves the normal calculated range, and 2 doubles each layer's deviation while the 1.00 µm anchor point remains fixed. The control accepts 0 through 2 in 0.001 increments.", "For example, a Fauxlographic anchor of 300 mm/s at 300 kHz produces a 1.00 µm pulse pitch, and a full 21-layer progression at Speed Spread 1 becomes 165, 180, 195, and so on through 465 mm/s.", {"before": "Rasterizer follows the fixed-frequency speed progression demonstrated in Ben Krasnow's ", "link_text": "MOPA Laser Diffraction Gratings reference project", "url": BEN_KRASNOW_DIFFRACTION_REPO, "after": ", then redistributes that progression across however many non-Black grating layers are available."}]),
        ("Calibration", ["Krasnow Grating does not require a fixed number of Rasterizer swatches or LightBurn layers. Each available non-Black LightBurn layer carries a separate grating level with its own calculated speed and microscopic pulse pitch. The LightBurn layer colors are organizational identifiers and do not predict the engraved color; the physical diffraction effect is determined by each layer's calculated speed-to-frequency ratio.", "In a Material Library-backed job, Preserve Black can keep source-derived Black geometry on the Black LightBurn layer using its matched setting. SVG-only output does not apply laser settings or produce a LightBurn project layer."]),
        ("Physical limitations", ["The calculation assumes that the selected Fauxlographic cut setting produces a 1.00 µm pulse pitch with the user's specific laser, lens, material, focus, and pulse behavior. Rasterizer cannot verify that calibration or compensate for nonlinear behavior from the laser, controller, optics, or material. At the default Speed Spread value of 1, calculated layer speeds range from 0.55× to 1.55× the Fauxlographic anchor speed. At the maximum value of 2, they range from 0.10× to 2.10× the anchor speed. Before producing final artwork, verify that every calculated speed is within your machine's operating limits, engrave a small pitch test, and inspect the result using the intended lighting and viewing positions.", "The default Gradient Top and Gradient Bottom values reproduce the approximate 165-to-90 vertical correction used in Ben Krasnow's reference project, but the required correction depends on the artwork's lighting and viewing geometry. Use Gradient Top, Gradient Bottom, and Gradient Curve to tune the correction for the artwork's intended size, orientation, lighting position, and viewing position."]),
    ],
    "related": ["geometry-styles", "color-layers", "diffraction-gratings", "holographic-calibration"],
})

# These controls are pipeline stages rather than abstract filters, so keep their
# hand-written guides beside the generated filter reference.
DOCS["artwork-cropping"] = _page(
    "Cropping Rasterizer Artwork Before Processing",
    "Crop rectangular, square, oval, circular, or transparent artwork in the browser before Rasterizer builds the quantized preview and output geometry.",
    "Rasterizer can create a new cropped source image in your browser before upload. When a crop is active, the cropped pixels, aspect ratio, transparency, quantized preview, SVG, and LightBurn project all follow that cropped source rather than the original file.",
    [
        ("Rectangle, square, oval, and circle crops", ["Choose a Crop shape and drag over the artwork. Drag inside the selection to move it, or drag a corner handle to resize it. Square and Circle keep equal dimensions; Rectangle and Oval can use different width and height.", "A valid selection applies automatically when the job is submitted. Crop preview applies it immediately so you can inspect the cropped source before submitting. Reset crop returns to the original uploaded artwork."]),
        ("Crop transparency", ["Crop transparency finds the smallest rectangular pixel bounds containing nontransparent artwork and creates a cropped PNG in the browser. Transparent pixels outside the artwork and transparent holes inside it remain transparent, and Rasterizer creates no geometry for those regions.", "Use this for logos, illustrations, or cutouts on a transparent canvas. It does not remove a white or checkerboard-looking background that has already been flattened into opaque pixels."]),
        ("Preview behavior", ["The quantized browser preview shows transparent regions over a checkerboard so they are not mistaken for Black or another palette color. The preview also shows millimeter rulers derived from the requested processing dimensions and Physical Pixel Size.", "The rulers communicate the calculated output dimensions, but a browser cannot guarantee that one on-screen millimeter equals one physical millimeter on every monitor. Browser zoom, operating-system scaling, pixel density, and display calibration affect the apparent size."]),
        ("Output dimensions", ["Cropping changes the source aspect ratio. As with an uncropped job, provide either processing width or processing height and leave the other at 0; Rasterizer calculates the missing dimension from the cropped aspect ratio. Physical Pixel Size then converts those processed pixels into the initial millimeter dimensions of the SVG and LightBurn project."]),
        ("Privacy and file handling", ["The crop operation and cropped preview are produced locally in the browser. Rasterizer uploads the resulting cropped file only when you submit the job. The original image is not additionally uploaded as part of that cropped submission."]),
    ],
    ["preset-controls", "geometry-styles", "raster-to-vector", "pixel-size", "color-layers"],
)

DOCS["geometry-styles"] = _page(
    "Geometry Styles and Per-Swatch Geometry Routing",
    "Apply Normal Vectors, Glyphs, Halftone Newsprint, or Krasnow Grating after compatible image processing, or assign a different treatment to each enabled Rasterizer swatch.",
    "Geometry Style is a separate Rasterizer pipeline stage. A compatible Image Style first prepares and color-separates the artwork; Geometry Style then keeps those regions as continuous vectors or rebuilds them as glyph, halftone, or diffraction-grating geometry.",
    [
        ("Normal Vectors", ["Normal Vectors preserves the continuous closed regions produced by the selected Image Style. Choose it when you do not want an additional glyph or grating transformation."]),
        ("Glyphs", ["Glyphs rebuilds the prepared color regions on one shared matrix using the selected solid shape. Available shapes include circle, square, diamond, triangle, hexagon, octagon, star, cross, bar, skull, heart, space invader, ghost, bat, alien head, paw print, fish scale, puzzle piece, a deterministic Mixed Shapes option, and a Custom Uploaded Glyph.", "Cell Size establishes the matrix spacing. Minimum and Maximum Glyph Ratio establish the permitted size range inside each cell. Non-Black Glyph Density, Tone Curve, Contrast, Grid Angle, Glyph Rotation, Random Rotation, and Seed further control the shared pattern. Black Only sends the marks to Black. Invert reverses the tone-to-size relationship. Invert Fill retains each color's vector regions and cuts glyph-shaped holes through them instead of replacing them with solid glyphs; it cannot be combined with Black Only."]),
        ("Glyph Size Source", ["Source Brightness sizes every glyph from the original artwork's grayscale brightness at that cell. The selected swatch still determines the output color and laser setting, but neighboring glyphs assigned to the same swatch can retain different sizes when their original source brightness differs.", "Seeded Variation assigns repeatable pseudo-random sizes across the Minimum and Maximum Glyph Ratio range. It can create different glyph sizes within areas that share one swatch as well as across different colors. The Seed makes the arrangement reproducible: the same artwork and settings produce the same variation, while changing the Seed produces another arrangement.", "Choose Source Brightness when glyph size should carry tonal detail from the image. Choose Seeded Variation when the palette colors already communicate the image and you want a more organic distribution of mark sizes."]),
        ("Halftone Newsprint", ["Halftone Newsprint rebuilds prepared color regions as circles or squares on one shared matrix. Source Brightness makes dot size carry the original grayscale tone within each swatch region. Seeded Variation instead selects repeatable sizes across the configured range, allowing varied marks of the same color without using tone as their size source.", "Black Only places every mark on Black for a conventional monochrome halftone. In Choose by swatch, Black remains Normal Vectors and routed Halftone marks retain their owning swatches, so Black Only is not offered in the shared routed settings."]),
        ("Custom uploaded glyphs", ["Choose Custom Uploaded Glyph under Glyph Shape, then upload an SVG, PNG, JPEG, or WebP. An SVG retains its closed vector paths. A raster image is converted to grayscale and traced into a silhouette using Threshold; Invert Custom Glyph reverses which side of that threshold becomes the mark.", "Padding reserves space around the normalized custom shape inside each glyph cell. Use a simple, high-contrast silhouette with enough internal clearance to survive at the chosen physical Cell Size. SVG text, linked content, scripts, embedded bitmap images, and open paths are not substitutes for closed vector geometry; convert intended lettering and strokes to closed paths before uploading.", "Inspect a small output at high zoom before engraving. Fine holes, narrow bridges, self-intersections, and tiny disconnected pieces can disappear or create excessive object counts when the custom shape is repeated many times."]),
        ("Tight Pack Geometry", ["With Tight Pack Geometry off, cells remain on the regular matrix established by Cell Size, Grid Angle, and the normal shape layout. With it on, Rasterizer uses a deterministic, collision-aware staggered layout and may move or rotate decorative glyphs or Krasnow cell boundaries independently to reduce unused space while keeping them inside their source-color ownership.", "Tight packing is most visible with decorative silhouettes that naturally leave substrate between neighboring cells. Gap-free shapes that already tessellate, such as the established square or hexagonal layouts, retain their normal layout. Tight packing does not change a glyph's assigned swatch or laser setting, and it does not replace the grating direction used inside a Krasnow cell.", "Packing is bounded rather than an assurance of mathematically perfect nesting. Compare the regular and packed results at the intended physical Cell or Patch Size, and inspect narrow gaps and near-touches in LightBurn before engraving."]),
        ("Independent random rotation", ["Random Rotation gives each generated Glyph or Krasnow cell its own deterministic orientation instead of rotating the complete matrix as one unit. The same Seed and job settings reproduce the same arrangement; changing Seed produces another arrangement.", "For Krasnow geometry, this rotates the cell boundary independently while the grating angle inside the cell still comes from the normal luminance and angle controls. Placements that would overlap are omitted so independent rotation does not create double-engraved cell footprints. Use a small physical test because irregular silhouettes can leave more exposed material and create narrow clearances."]),
        ("Krasnow Grating", ["Krasnow Grating converts the prepared regions into clipped cells of open diffraction-grating lines. It follows any compatible Image Style, including No geometric effect when minimal preprocessing is desired.", "Choose Custom under Cell Shape to upload an SVG made from one or more closed vector paths. Padding reserves clearance around the normalized boundary. Open paths, text, images, scripts, and linked content are not supported; convert the intended silhouette to closed paths before upload.", "Fauxlogram Gradient Scope and Direction can span the entire artwork or restart inside each disconnected prepared shape, and can run vertically, horizontally, or radially."]),
        ("Choose by swatch", ["Choose by swatch lets each enabled palette swatch use Normal Vectors, Glyphs, Halftone Newsprint, or Krasnow Grating. Select swatches and use the bulk assignment buttons, or change individual assignments in the swatch grid. Shared settings appear whenever at least one swatch uses the corresponding treatment.", "Each prepared source region receives one treatment, and Rasterizer makes output layers mutually exclusive before export. Black remains Normal Vectors in this mixed-routing mode."]),
        ("Compatibility", ["Geometry Style is available for standard and ordinary abstract image processing. Optical Color Mix already constructs final mixed geometry and therefore does not accept another Geometry Style transformation."]),
        ("Settings and persistence", ["Signed-in members' most recently submitted Geometry Style, per-swatch assignments, and shared Glyph, Halftone, or Krasnow settings are restored with their Rasterizer workflow settings. Guest sessions use the form defaults and do not save account-backed preferences."]),
    ],
    ["preset-controls", "artwork-cropping", "panel-tiling", "halftone-newsprint-geometry", "krasnow-grating-geometry", "color-layers"],
)

DOCS["panel-tiling"] = _page(
    "Panel Tiling for Multi-Blank Engravings",
    "Split one finished Rasterizer design across repeatable material blanks, with optional assembly gaps, safe edge insets, workbed-centered tile files, and an assembly map.",
    "Panel Tiling is an output-layout stage applied after image processing, color separation, geometry routing, and overlap resolution. Rasterizer first builds the complete assembled design, then clips that finalized geometry into individual tile files. Every blank can therefore be placed at the same repeatable position on the laser workbed while its file engraves the correct portion of the larger composition.",
    [
        ("When to use Panel Tiling", ["Use Panel Tiling when the assembled sign, artwork, wall piece, or panel composition is larger than one material blank or the laser's usable field. It is also useful when a design intentionally spans separate metal, wood, acrylic, or other compatible pieces mounted with visible spacing between them.", "Panel Tiling does not increase the laser's physical field and does not automate material registration. It prepares separate, consistently positioned files; the operator still needs repeatable blanks, a reliable fixture, and a proven origin and focus procedure."]),
        ("Define the assembled layout", ["Enable Panel Tiling under Output Layout, then enter the physical Tile width and Tile height of each blank and the desired number of Columns and Rows. Rasterizer scales the complete source artwork to the assembled span before it clips individual panels.", "Horizontal and Vertical gap describe intentional empty assembly distance between neighboring blanks. Set both gaps to 0 for edge-to-edge, seamless panel coordinates. A nonzero gap reserves that distance in the complete composition, so artwork that would fall in the gap is not placed on either adjacent blank.", "Edge inset creates an unengraved safety margin inside every blank. It is separate from the gap: a gap lies between blanks in the assembled design, while an inset trims artwork away from all four physical edges of each blank."]),
        ("Describe the laser workbed", ["Workbed width and Workbed height describe the usable coordinate area in the generated tile projects. Each tile's physical blank is centered at half of those dimensions: X equals Workbed width divided by 2, and Y equals Workbed height divided by 2.", "The tile dimensions must fit inside the described workbed. Use values that match the coordinate system and field configuration you will open in LightBurn, then verify the resulting position with Frame or another safe alignment procedure before running the laser."]),
        ("Choose tile order and identifiers", ["Row major numbers tiles across each row before moving to the next row. Column major numbers down each column before moving to the next column. Serpentine alternates direction on each row, which can make physical installation or batch handling easier.", "Include tile IDs adds identifiers to the assembly map. The generated filenames always include sequence, row, and column coordinates, such as tile-01-r01-c01, so retain those names until assembly is complete."]),
        ("Understand the downloads", ["The job's panel ZIP contains one SVG for every tile and, for a Material Library-backed job, a corresponding LightBurn project for every tile. It also contains panel-manifest.json with the layout, assembled dimensions, source position, and workbed position for each tile, plus panel-assembly.svg as a visual placement map.", "The ordinary complete-artwork outputs remain useful for review, but use the individual tile files for the repeated-blank workflow. SVG tile files contain geometry without laser settings; the tile .lbrn2 files carry the applicable settings copied from the selected Material Library or saved palette."]),
        ("Alignment and assembly workflow", ["Make or secure a fixture that locates every same-size blank at the identical workbed position. Mark each blank with its tile identifier outside the finished face or track it on a separate assembly diagram. Load the matching tile project, confirm the device and origin, frame safely, engrave, allow the material to cool, then replace the blank without changing the fixture.", "Arrange the finished blanks according to panel-assembly.svg. Zero gaps produce adjoining design coordinates, but a visually seamless result still depends on accurate blank dimensions, square edges, fixture repeatability, focus, material consistency, and assembly tolerances. Test a small multi-panel alignment pattern before committing valuable material."]),
        ("Limits and verification", ["Rows and columns may each range from 1 to 20, with no more than 100 tiles in one job. Tile and workbed dimensions must be between 1 and 3000 mm, while horizontal and vertical gaps must be between 0 and 1000 mm. Edge inset must leave a positive engravable area inside the tile. The assembled panel must also remain within Rasterizer's 1,600-processing-pixel limit on each axis, so very large physical layouts require a correspondingly larger Pixel size.", "Open several tile files before production and verify their scale, workbed-centered position, layer settings, edge clipping, inset, order, and continuity against the assembly map. Generated placement is not a substitute for machine safety checks, suitable guarding and extraction, material testing, or direct supervision."]),
    ],
    ["preset-controls", "geometry-styles", "lightburn-export", "why-fixturing-is-so-important", "job-history"],
)

DOCS["krasnow-grating-geometry"].update({
    "description": "Create open-path diffraction-grating artwork from palette-mapped regions, using the selected material's Fauxlographic setting as the machine-specific carrier-setting anchor.",
    "intro": "Krasnow Grating is a Geometry Style applied after a compatible Image Style prepares and color-separates the source. It replaces selected regions with clipped cells of parallel open lines. Source luminance controls line angle; source hue and the fauxlogram gradient select grating carriers. It supports artwork-wide or shape-local vertical, horizontal, and radial gradient layouts.",
    "sections": [
        ("Preserve Black", ["With Preserve Black enabled, source-derived Black remains ordinary closed Black geometry using the matched Black Cut Setting, while other regions become gratings. With Preserve Black disabled, Black is treated like the other colors: its regions become grating lines and its LightBurn layer receives a clone of the Fauxlographic carrier setting.", "The output does not place a full Black backing shape underneath the grating layers. Prepared regions have exclusive ownership so multiple laser settings do not engrave the same footprint."]),
        ("Cell shapes and packing", ["Gap-free layouts include Square, Hexagon, Triangle, Diamond / Rhombus, and Puzzle Piece. The staggered Skull, Heart, Space Invader, Ghost, Bat, Alien Head, Paw Print, and Fish Scale layouts intentionally expose substrate between silhouettes. Custom accepts an uploaded SVG made from closed vector paths; Padding reserves clearance around its normalized boundary. Patch Size determines the physical cell scale and is independent of processing pixel size.", "Tight Pack Geometry uses deterministic collision-aware placement to reduce empty space around compatible decorative cells. Random Rotation gives individual cells reproducible orientations from Seed; the grating direction inside each cell remains controlled separately by the angle calculation. Overlapping placements are omitted.", "When a cell covers multiple processed pixels, the prepared region and source samples within that cell determine its clipped ownership, angle, and carrier selection. Smaller cells retain more local variation but generate more geometry. Inspect the checkerboard custom-shape preview and a small LightBurn output before using detailed silhouettes at production scale."]),
        ("Visible line spacing", ["Line Spacing controls the visible distance between the parallel paths inside a cell. Hue Line Spacing Minimum and Maximum optionally vary that visible density by approximate wavelength order: violet and blue lie near the minimum and red near the maximum; purple and magenta follow a continuous violet-to-red proxy. Equal minimum and maximum values preserve uniform spacing, and low-saturation colors use Line Spacing.", "These visible hatch distances are separate from the microscopic along-path pulse pitch created by laser speed and frequency."]),
        ("Angle and color routing", ["Source luminance maps between Angle Minimum and Angle Maximum. For sufficiently saturated source colors, Hue Rotation and Ben Krasnow's inverse hue mapping contribute to the carrier-layer selection. The gradient endpoints and Gradient Curve add a position-dependent offset. Colors at or below Saturation Cutoff omit the hue contribution and use only that gradient.", "Fauxlogram Gradient Start and End define the carrier progression, while Scope and Direction determine where and how it is evaluated."]),
        ("Fauxlogram Gradient controls in Geometry Style", ["Fauxlogram Gradient Scope offers Entire Artwork and Each Shape. Entire Artwork evaluates every grating cell against the bounds of the complete prepared artwork. Each Shape restarts the progression inside every disconnected prepared vector component, so separate objects can each receive a complete gradient.", "Fauxlogram Gradient Direction offers Top to Bottom, Bottom to Top, Left to Right, Right to Left, Center to Edge, and Edge to Center. The radial choices use an elliptical normalized distance from the relevant artwork or shape center, so whole-artwork radial layouts are supported as well as shape-local radial layouts.", "The Start and End values define the two ends of the carrier-selection offset, while Gradient Curve redistributes the transition between them. These controls change carrier-layer selection; they do not rotate the parallel grating paths. Angle Minimum and Angle Maximum still control line orientation. Touching regions that have already merged into one prepared component are treated as one shape, and very small shapes may contain too few cells to display a smooth progression."]),
        ("Fauxlographic carrier setting", ["The selected material must contain a Cut Setting Entry identified as Fauxlographic. Rasterizer uses the Fauxlographic setting as the authoritative template and clones its power, frequency, pulse width, passes, and other applicable settings onto the grating carrier layers. Additional sublayers, if present, are ignored because the generated diffraction gratings rely on one precise Cut/Line operation. The clone retains each destination layer's LightBurn identity; calculated speed is adjusted for its target pulse pitch.", "If a source field such as q-pulse width is genuinely absent, Rasterizer leaves it absent rather than inventing a device default. LightBurn may display a device-dependent value after opening the project."]),
        ("Pulse pitch and Speed Spread", ["Along-path pulse pitch is speed divided by frequency. Rasterizer treats the Fauxlographic anchor speed and frequency as a machine-specific 1.00 µm reference and distributes target pitches across the available grating carriers. Speed Spread controls their divergence from that anchor: 0 keeps every carrier at the anchor speed, 1 uses the normal range, and 2 doubles the deviation.", "The organizational RGB color of a LightBurn layer does not itself predict the physical diffraction color. Material, focus, optics, pulse behavior, illumination, and viewing angle all affect the observed result."]),
        ("Where it belongs in the pipeline", ["Select Krasnow Grating under Geometry Style. Use No geometric effect when you want minimal image transformation before the grating stage, or choose another compatible Image Style when it should deliberately transform the regions first. Choose by swatch when only selected colors should become gratings."]),
        ("Physical validation", ["The calculations cannot verify that a particular machine and material produce the assumed diffraction response. Confirm every generated layer and setting in LightBurn, check that calculated speeds and frequencies remain within machine limits, and engrave a small test under the intended focus, lighting, and viewing conditions.", {"before": "The approach is based on Ben Krasnow's ", "link_text": "MOPA Laser Diffraction Gratings reference project", "url": BEN_KRASNOW_DIFFRACTION_REPO, "after": ", adapted to the selected Rasterizer palette and Fauxlographic setting."}]),
    ],
    "related": ["geometry-styles", "color-layers", "diffraction-gratings", "holographic-calibration"],
})

DOCS["lightburn-export"].update({
    "description": "Understand Rasterizer SVG and LightBurn project downloads, embedded job notes, and the warning added to especially large .lbrn2 files.",
    "intro": "Material Library-backed jobs provide both an SVG and a LightBurn .lbrn2 project; download whichever output files you want or need for your workflow. SVG-only jobs provide just the layered SVG. Download buttons show Download followed by the output file's basename and extension, without exposing storage paths.",
    "sections": [
        ("SVG versus LightBurn project", ["The SVG contains portable color-separated vector geometry but no LightBurn laser settings. The .lbrn2 project contains the corresponding LightBurn layers and applicable settings copied from the selected Material Library or palette."]),
        ("Embedded project Notes", ["Every Rasterizer-generated .lbrn2 project includes a LightBurn Notes entry recording the job type, Rasterizer parameters, abstract filter and its parameters, Geometry Style and its parameters, selected material, color-matching controls, and used swatches. Private internal-only parameters are excluded.", "These notes make an exported project easier to inspect later, but Job History remains the account-backed record for signed-in members."]),
        ("Projects larger than 50 MB", ["After serialization, a .lbrn2 file larger than 50,000,000 bytes receives a warning at the top of its Notes and is configured to show that note when loaded. The warning explains that LightBurn can appear frozen or Not Responding after Frame, Send, or Start while it calculates dense geometry.", "Rasterizer-generated LightBurn projects retain Order by Layer as the only ordering rule and automatically disable the remaining path optimizations, so no manual optimization change is required. Be patient while LightBurn completes its calculations, and review the dedicated large-project guide before operating an unusually dense file."]),
        ("Before engraving", ["Open the .lbrn2 file in LightBurn and verify dimensions, origin, layer order, cut mode, speed, power, frequency, pulse width, passes, line interval, hatch behavior, and Notes. For SVG-only output, import the SVG into the software appropriate for your machine and assign every production setting there."]),
        ("Safety", ["Generated geometry and copied settings are not an assurance that a job is safe. Confirm machine limits, material compatibility, focus, extraction, supervision, and fire-control procedures before running the laser."]),
    ],
    "related": ["lightburn-large-projects", "svg-only-mode", "works-with-lightburn", "job-history", "material-libraries", "geometry-styles"],
})

